<p>The Ethics of Ambiguity, Part One</p>
<p><img src="/phil2306/assets/images/gettyimages-1176351392-612x612.jpg" alt="Photograph of a Paris street scene in the 1940s with demonstrators marching." width="860" height="320" loading="lazy" class="page-hero-image"></p>
<div class="callout callout--concept"><span class="callout__icon" aria-hidden="true">💡</span><div class="callout__content">
<p>Part One asks what kind of being a human being is, before asking what a human being ought to do.</p>
</div></div>
<div class="accordion">
<div class="accordion__item">
  <button class="accordion__header" aria-expanded="false" type="button">
    <span class="accordion__number">1</span>
    Existence precedes essence
    <span class="accordion__chevron" aria-hidden="true">▼</span>
  </button>
  <div class="accordion__content">
    <div class="accordion__body">
<p>De Beauvoir's argument in Part One rests on a distinctly existentialist starting point: that human beings have no predetermined essence. This is the claim Sartre had made famous with the slogan "existence precedes essence." For most things in the world, essence precedes existence: a hammer is designed to be a hammer before it is made; a chair is conceived as a chair before it is built. But a human being is not made according to a blueprint. There is no designer, no divine plan, no rational structure of the will that fixes in advance what a human being is supposed to be and do. We exist first, and only then do we create, through our choices and actions, what we are.</p>
<p>The implications of this for morality are profound. Every previous thinker in this course assumed that morality has a foundation in something outside and prior to human choice: human nature (Aristotle), divine and natural law (Aquinas), pure reason (Kant), the facts of happiness (Mill). Even Nietzsche, who challenged all of these foundations, did not attempt to build a positive ethics without them. De Beauvoir takes the foundationless condition seriously and asks: given that we have no essence, no predetermined nature, no external moral authority, what does ethics look like?</p>
<p>Her answer begins with a clarification about what freedom means in this context. Freedom, for de Beauvoir (following Sartre), is not the freedom to do whatever you want, unconstrained by circumstance. That kind of freedom is a fantasy. We are always situated: born into a particular body, a particular time, a particular language, a particular class and race and sex. We did not choose these conditions and cannot simply step outside them. The freedom de Beauvoir is talking about is not freedom from situation but freedom within situation: the irreducible capacity to take up one's situation, to interpret it, to project beyond it toward possibilities it does not dictate. Even the prisoner in a cell is free in this sense, because the prisoner's response to imprisonment, their inner orientation toward it, is not determined by the fact of the cell.</p>
<p>This is a demanding conception of freedom, because it means that freedom cannot be escaped. You cannot choose not to be free. Even the refusal to choose, the passive drifting, is a choice. The person who says "I have no choice" in most cases does have choices; they have simply decided to act as if they do not. That decision is itself an exercise of freedom, and de Beauvoir calls the refusal to acknowledge it "bad faith."</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>
<div class="accordion__item">
  <button class="accordion__header" aria-expanded="false" type="button">
    <span class="accordion__number">2</span>
    The ambiguity of the human condition
    <span class="accordion__chevron" aria-hidden="true">▼</span>
  </button>
  <div class="accordion__content">
    <div class="accordion__body">
<p>The central concept of the book is "ambiguity," and it is worth being precise about what de Beauvoir means by it. She is not using "ambiguity" loosely to mean vagueness or uncertainty. She is describing something specific and structural about human existence.</p>
<p>A human being is simultaneously two things that seem incompatible. On one side, we are free consciousnesses: beings who are not reducible to our circumstances, who can step back from any given situation and take up a stance toward it. We are not rocks or plants, driven entirely by forces outside our control. On the other side, we are thoroughly embedded beings: material, historical, social. We have bodies that age and get sick and die. We are shaped by the economic and historical conditions of our birth. We depend on other people. We live in time and cannot step outside it. We are, in short, not pure freedom but freedom situated in the world.</p>
<p>These two aspects of human existence pull against each other and cannot be resolved. We cannot become pure freedom, pure transcendence, because we always have a situation we are transcending from. We cannot collapse into pure facticity, pure thing-hood, because we always remain free in the sense of taking some stance toward our situation. We are stuck in the tension, and this is the ambiguity de Beauvoir thinks every honest ethics must begin from.</p>
<p>The failure to hold this tension honestly is what produces bad faith. On one side, you can pretend to be more free than you are: imagining that your choices have no constraints, that you can simply will yourself into being anything at all, ignoring the weight of history and circumstance. On the other side, you can pretend to be less free than you are: claiming to have no choices, reducing yourself to a product of your circumstances, acting as though your situation determines your responses without remainder. Both are escapes from the burden of living in the ambiguity honestly, and both fail because the ambiguity cannot actually be escaped, only evaded.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>
<div class="accordion__item">
  <button class="accordion__header" aria-expanded="false" type="button">
    <span class="accordion__number">3</span>
    Freedom and responsibility
    <span class="accordion__chevron" aria-hidden="true">▼</span>
  </button>
  <div class="accordion__content">
    <div class="accordion__body">
<p>If freedom is inescapable, so is responsibility. This is the existentialist claim that follows directly from the premises. Because I cannot not choose, because even inaction is a form of action, I cannot avoid being responsible for what my choices produce. There is no alibi available. "I was following orders" is not an excuse; it describes a choice made under pressure, but a choice nonetheless. "It was the way I was raised" is not an excuse; my upbringing shapes my starting point but does not dictate my responses. "Everyone does it" is not an excuse; the fact that a behavior is widespread does not remove my responsibility for joining in.</p>
<p>This is a demanding position, and it is worth acknowledging that it has been criticized. Some philosophers have argued that de Beauvoir and Sartre overstate the degree to which human beings are free, understating the degree to which psychological compulsions, social structures, and material conditions genuinely constrain choice. The prisoner analogy works for some cases but strains under others: a person systematically denied education, healthcare, and economic opportunity is not simply making a different choice about their situation in the way a prisoner is. De Beauvoir herself was sensitive to this objection, and her later work engages it more directly. The important point for reading Part One is to understand the starting position clearly before evaluating it.</p>
<p>The ethical upshot of radical freedom and radical responsibility is that we cannot appeal to external authority to validate our choices. There is no God to tell us what to do, no natural law to read off from human nature, no categorical imperative given by pure reason, no utilitarian calculus that spits out a verdict. We have to choose, and we have to own the choice. This is what de Beauvoir calls "assuming" one's freedom: not just acknowledging it abstractly but living it, accepting the full weight of what it means to be the kind of being that creates its own values rather than discovering them ready-made.</p>
<div class="callout callout--connection"><span class="callout__icon" aria-hidden="true">➡️</span><div class="callout__content">
<p>← LOOKING BACK</p>
<p>Compare de Beauvoir's starting point with each of the earlier thinkers. Aristotle appealed to human nature as a given foundation. Aquinas appealed to eternal law. Kant appealed to pure reason. Mill appealed to the facts of happiness. All of them found something outside and prior to choice to anchor morality. De Beauvoir starts with choice itself as the only certainty, and asks what follows from there. Part Two develops the answer.</p>
</div></div>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>
<div class="accordion__item">
  <button class="accordion__header" aria-expanded="false" type="button">
    <span class="accordion__number">4</span>
    How freedom can generate ethics
    <span class="accordion__chevron" aria-hidden="true">▼</span>
  </button>
  <div class="accordion__content">
    <div class="accordion__body">
<p>The most pressing question Part One raises but does not fully answer is: if there are no fixed values, if everything is a product of choice, how can any choices be better or worse than others? This is the challenge usually pressed against existentialist ethics: without a standard outside choice, isn't every choice equally valid? If I choose to live selfishly and you choose to live generously, is there any philosophical basis for preferring one over the other?</p>
<p>De Beauvoir's response begins to take shape at the end of Part One and is developed fully in Part Two. Her answer is not that there are fixed values after all. It is that not every way of living honestly confronts the condition of freedom. Some ways of living deny freedom, either one's own or others'. And that denial is a kind of failure on existentialist grounds themselves, not by appeal to any external moral standard.</p>
<p>Consider: if freedom is what we irreducibly are, then a life lived in bad faith, a life that systematically refuses to acknowledge its own freedom, is a life that fails on its own terms. It is not merely different from a life lived authentically; it is self-contradictory. The person in bad faith acts as if they have no choices while constantly exercising the choice to act as if they have no choices. This internal incoherence is not a neutral alternative; it is a failure of integrity in the most literal sense: the person is not integrated, not whole, not honest about what they are.</p>
<p>This is de Beauvoir's first move toward ethics: the claim that authentic freedom, freedom honestly assumed, has a certain structure, and that violating that structure is a form of failure that can be evaluated even without appealing to external moral standards. Part Two works out what that structure is and what it demands from us regarding others.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>
